C:/DocumentNoSnc/cc/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC\regress\artifacts\lcaisalane\f2_8bd97e4_20260918\panel_20260918_190129\fable5_f2.md:37:- **"All seven guard-variants are L2" (glm, deepseek, minimax)** over-counts. They are right that you cannot silently narrow an overclaiming contract, and right that F5's *remedy wording* is laundering-shaped. They are wrong that `va/vb/ve/vg/vt1/vt2` need rows. Only `vc`'s operand (lane) is body-distinguished. Width thresholds (`va/vb`) are a partition the body never tests (it tests `len` only against 0); port (`ve`) is a passthrough into the forward address, not a tested partition — that is the parent's already-ruled `p_port10` class; `k`/a<4:0> (`vg/vt1`) is discarded by `>>5` — the parent's already-ruled `lcaisasize` class; chunk-position (`vt2`) is not a body-read lvalue (the handler is stateless across calls). I confirmed `p_port10` and `lcaisasize` are pre-existing flagship-ruled disclosures in `lca_isalane_probe.py:196,199,209`, measured 51/51 and 93/93 — so disclosing these six is citing settled classes, **not** inventing an escape hatch for this measurement. That is the line between honest disclosure and laundering.
